OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774228187
OL,1,Vk4                               G4/20
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGUK TRADE FIGURES WORSE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGTHAN EXPECTED
OL,4,F
OL,5, Britain's current account deficit
OL,6, improved slightly last month, but the
OL,7, figures were worse than expected.
OL,9,FAnalysts had predicted a sharp fall
OL,10,Fto #450-500 million from a revised
OL,11,FJanuary figure of #858m but the figure
OL,12,Fonly dropped to #750m.
OL,14,FThe visible trade gap was #1.05 billion
OL,15,Fcompared with #1.16bn in January. The
OL,16,Fpredicted figure was about #900m.
OL,17,F
OL,18,FInvisible earnings were projected to be
OL,19,F#300m compared to #600m in the last
OL,20,Fthree months of 1992.
OL,21,F
OL,22,D] FThis Ceefax page is fromG23-03-92
OL,23,D] FMuch more at: teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49605
SC,0005
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774228187
OL,1,Vk4                               G5/20
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGTRADE FIGURES "SHOW
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGRECOVERY ON THE WAY"
OL,4,F
OL,5, The Prime Minister says today's trade
OL,6, figures suggest that economic recovery
OL,7, is on the way because of the increase
OL,8, in imports in capital goods.
OL,10,FThat could be attributed to companies
OL,11,Finvesting in plant equipment, he said.
OL,12,F
OL,13,FLabour leader Neil Kinnock, however,
OL,14,Fsaid the trade figures were "quite
OL,15,Fdreadful" and about twice as bad as any
OL,16,Fof the analysts had expected.
OL,18,FLib Dems' trade spokesman Alex Carlile
OL,19,Fsaid the figures showed the Tories had
OL,20,Fdone nothing to create the necessary
OL,21,Fbasis for a manufacturing industry.
OL,23,D]FFromG23-03-92F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49606
SC,0006
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774228187
OL,1,Vk4                               G6/20
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGMAJOR QUESTIONED
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGOVER TAX CLAIM
OL,4,D
OL,5, The Prime Minister has been challenged
OL,6, over the Tories' claim to be the party
OL,7, of low taxation.
OL,9,FMr Major said that the overall tax
OL,10,Fburden had remained "broadly flat"
OL,11,Funder the Conservatives.
OL,12,F
OL,13,FBut this contradicted Treasury figures
OL,14,Fshowing a tax burden of 34.5p in the
OL,15,Fpound in 1978-79 and 36.5p in 1991-92.
OL,16,F
OL,17,FShadow chancellor John Smith said Mr
OL,18,FMajor should admit he was wrong; the
OL,19,FChancellor had got the tax burden down
OL,20,Fby borrowing, but the figure was still
OL,21,Fhigher than the Tories had inherited.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG23-03-92F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49607
SC,0007
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774228187
OL,1,Vk4                               G7/20
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGMAJOR DENIES TAX RISE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGNEWSPAPER CLAIM
OL,4,D
OL,5, John Major has denied a claim in The
OL,6, Guardian that the next government would
OL,7, have to raise taxes because the economy
OL,8, will be so far in the red.
OL,10,FMr Major said the paper had got several
OL,11,Fof its figures incorrect and was wrong
OL,12,Fin its suggestion that current spending
OL,13,Fwould not be covered by tax revenues.
OL,15,FLabour leader Neil Kinnock, however,
OL,16,Fsaid that Chancellor Norman Lamont
OL,17,Fshould resign over the issue.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FHe said that in his Budget, Mr Lamont
OL,20,Fhad borrowed to finance tax cuts while
OL,21,Fknowing expenditure exceeded receipts.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG23-03-92F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
